ctiveness of different treatments, interventions, or conditions.\n\n### Formula\nThe formula for the Z-test statistic is given by:\n\n\\[\nZ = \\frac{\\bar{X_1} - \\bar{X_2}}{\\sqrt{\\frac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1} + \\frac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( \\bar{X_1} \\) and \\( \\bar{X_2} \\) are the sample means of the two groups,\n- \\( \\sigma_1^2 \\) and \\( \\sigma_2^2 \\) are the population variances,\n- \\( n_1 \\) and \\( n_2 \\) are the sample sizes of the two groups.\n\n### Assumptions\nThe Z-test relies on several key assumptions:\n1. **Normality**: The data in each group should be approximately normally distributed, especially important when sample sizes are small. However, with larger samples, the Central Limit Theorem suggests that the sampling distribution of the mean will be approximately normal regardless of the shape of the population distribution.\n2. **Independence**: The observations within each group must be independent of each other. This means that the selection of one observation does not influence the selection of another.\n3. **Known Variance**: The population variances of the two groups should be known or can be reliably estimated. If the variances are unknown, alternative tests such as the t-test may be more appropriate.\n\n### Importance\nThe Z-test is significant in hypothesis testing as it provides a method for determining whether observed differences between group means are statistically significant or could have occurred by random chance. This is crucial for making informed decisions based on empirical data.\n\n### Interpretation\nUpon calculating the Z-test statistic, it is compared against critical values from the standard normal distribution to determine the p-value. A low p-value (typically less than 0.05) indicates that the null hypothesis (which posits no difference between the group means) can be rejected, suggesting that there is a statistically significant difference between the means of the two groups.\n\n### Consequence